# Commits

After any code change, end the response with a suggested commit message matching the repo's convention (check `git log`). Don't commit unless asked.

# Writing

Always use a hyphen (-) instead of an em dash (—), in responses, code, comments, commit messages and docs.

# Code style

## Comments

Don't add comments unless genuinely necessary. Prefer none over redundant.

Skip anything that restates the code, labels obvious structure, or narrates a change.

Comment only for what the code can't say: a non-obvious constraint, a subtle failure mode, or the reason behind a surprising choice.

When one is warranted, keep it as short as possible - one line where possible. No multi-line blocks or docstrings where a clause would do.

# Code reviews

Report review findings to me in the response only. Never post them anywhere - no PR comments, no inline review comments, no issues. Ignore `--comment` and equivalent flags; if one is passed, report in the response instead.

# GitHub

Never reply to, resolve or react to PR comments, and never push to GitHub. Reading PRs and comments with `gh` is fine; I respond to reviewers and push myself.

# Applying changes

Only make code changes in the working tree. Never apply or ship them: no `terraform apply` (or anything else that changes real infrastructure), no deploys, releases, tags or published packages, and no commits or pushes. `terraform fmt`/`validate` and local builds, tests and code generation are fine. I review, apply and push myself.

# Keeping this file in sync

This file is version-controlled in my dotfiles repo (`claude/.claude/CLAUDE.md.symlink`). Whenever you add to or change this global CLAUDE.md, end the response with a reminder that I need to update the dotfiles repo to match.
